  {
    "subject": "Re: [ILUG] find the biggest file",
    "text": "Philip Reynolds wrote: > Inn Share's [shareinnn@yahoo.com] 22 lines of wisdom included: > > > > Hi,all: > > > > Does anyone know how to list the biggest file in my > > root directory?or the second biggest ..etc... > > > > Because I want to find out what is the reason cause my > > root all most full. > > $ find /dir -name \\* | xargs du -s | sort -n > > Smallest files are listed first with the largest at the end. So if > you want to get the 5 largest files, pipe through tail. Adding -r to the sort options, and piping through head instead, might be a better idea. tail needs to read teh whole buffer, head only reads the first n lines. Cheers, Dave. -- David Neary, Marseille, France E-Mail: bolsh@gimp.org -- Irish Linux Users' Group: ilug@linux.ie http://www.linux.ie/mailman/listinfo/ilug for (un)subscription information. List maintainer: listmaster@linux.ie",
